## Analysis log
One entry per numbered step: what was done, the decision and why, the output, and what it
means. Entries are added as each step is completed and approved.

<!-- ANALYSIS-LOG-START -->

### Step 1 — Data acquisition
Downloaded GSE120575 from NCBI GEO by hand: the TPM matrix and the per-cell annotation, two
files totalling 120.93 MiB, both verified against published SHA-256 digests.
**Decision:** use the GEO processed matrix, because the raw sequencing reads were deposited in
dbGaP under `phs001680.v1.p1` (controlled access) and cannot be obtained here.
**Output:** `data/raw/` holds the two `.gz` files. **Means:** the analysis begins from processed
expression values, so no step upstream of quantification is reproducible from this repo.

### Step 2 — Load and inspect
Parsed both files without modifying them. **Decision:** write a custom parser rather than use
`read_csv` defaults, because the matrix has *two* header rows (cell barcode, then sample label)
and a trailing tab on every data row, and the annotation file is a filled-in GEO submission
spreadsheet whose real header is on line 19.
**Output:** 55,737 genes x 16,291 cells; annotation of 16,291 rows x 11 columns, in the same
cell order as the matrix. **Means:** the two files describe the same cells and can be joined by
position, which later steps rely on.

### Step 3 — Cohort characterisation
Tabulated the annotation. **Decision:** treat the annotation's patient/sample column (48 levels)
as the biological sample unit and the matrix's second header row (57 levels) as the sequencing
unit, rather than assuming they agree — they disagree for 991 cells.
**Output:** 48 samples, of which nine post-treatment samples were split into `_T_enriched` and
`_myeloid_enriched` sort fractions; 17 responder vs 31 non-responder samples (5,564 vs 10,727
cells); therapy 35 anti-PD1, 11 anti-CTLA4+PD1, 2 anti-CTLA4.
**Means:** cell-type *proportions* cannot be compared naively across samples whose composition
was set by sorting, and pre/post biopsies from the same patients make cells non-independent.

### Step 4 — QC metrics and transform identification
Computed per-cell and per-gene detection, then identified the value scale.
**Decision:** test the transform against an invariant rather than infer it from the filename —
TPM must sum to 10^6 per cell, so un-logging with 2^x - 1 either reproduces that or it does not.
**Output:** un-logged column sums have median 994,726 with 94.7% of cells within 1% of 10^6,
confirming the values are log2(TPM+1), not linear TPM. Mitochondrial fraction on the 13
protein-coding MT genes has median 2.90%; on all 37 MT genes (including rRNA and tRNA) it is
12.72%. **Means:** the data must not be log-transformed again, and the mitochondrial QC
threshold is only meaningful against the protein-coding definition.

### Step 5 — Build the working matrix
Streamed the gzip in 500-gene blocks into a single sparse matrix.
**Decision:** store sparse and keep all genes, because the matrix is only 4.32% non-zero, so
sparse storage needs ~300 MB against 3.38 GB dense — filtering genes was never needed to fit in
memory. **Output:** 55,737 x 16,291, 39,238,221 non-zeros, 138 MiB on disk; gene count and
non-zero count both matched the independent first pass exactly. **Means:** all later steps load
this instead of re-reading the raw file, which takes 20-40 minutes per pass.

### Step 6 — QC filtering
Applied mitochondrial fraction <= 10% on the 13 protein-coding MT genes, a floor of 200 detected
genes, and a requirement that genes be detected in at least 3 cells.
**Decision:** drop the `max_genes_per_cell: 6000` cap, because it is a droplet-data doublet
heuristic and this is plate-based Smart-seq2 with one cell per well.
**Output:** 16,291 -> 16,048 cells (243 removed, all on mitochondrial content) and 55,737 ->
45,686 genes, retaining 98.85% of all non-zero values. Removal was even across arms: 1.64% of
responder vs 1.42% of non-responder cells. **Means:** filtering did not bias the comparison of
interest. Doublets were not screened for — plate-based data needs a different approach than the
standard droplet detectors, so this is unassessed rather than clean.

### Step 7 — Normalization
No normalization applied. **Decision:** skip it deliberately — TPM already is library-size
normalization and step 4 verified the log transform, so running the usual
`normalize_total` + `log1p` would double-normalize and double-log the data.
**Output:** gene means span 0.0001-16.41 and variances 4.3e-05-32.23, consistent with
log2(TPM+1). **Means:** the only normalization choice remaining was whether to z-score genes
before PCA, which was done in step 9.

### Step 8 — Feature selection (highly variable genes)
Selected 2,000 genes by binned normalised dispersion. Took three attempts; the first two were
wrong and were caught by inspecting which genes had been chosen.
**Decision:** restrict candidates to genes detected in at least 1% of cells and use
equal-frequency bins. Computing dispersion on log values (attempt 1) selected lncRNAs and
mitochondrial tRNAs; computing it correctly but with equal-width bins (attempt 2) still failed,
because one bin held 61.7% of all genes and so normalised nothing.
**Output:** 2,000 genes from 17,685 candidates, median detection 333 cells, including `GZMB`,
`NKG7`, `LYZ`, `CD14`, `MS4A1`, `CD79A`, `PMEL`. **Means:** the gene set now reflects real
cell-to-cell variation. 153 of the 2,000 are T/B-cell receptor V(D)J segments, which track
clonotype rather than cell state — an open caveat carried into clustering.

### Step 9 — Dimensionality reduction (PCA)
Z-scored the 2,000 genes across cells, clipped at +/-10, and took 30 components.
**Decision:** compute PCA exactly via eigendecomposition of the gene covariance matrix rather
than an approximate solver, since at 2,000 genes it is cheap and avoids a randomness parameter.
**Output:** PC1 explains 3.31%, PC2 1.49%, all 30 together 11.8%, with no sharp elbow;
loadings orthonormal to machine precision. PC1 correlates +0.955 with a myeloid marker score and
PC2 -0.864 with a B-cell score. **Means:** the dominant structure is immune lineage, as expected
for CD45+ cells. Two caveats: PC1 also correlates +0.61 with genes detected per cell, so it
mixes biology with library complexity; and response does not separate in the top components,
which is expected because response is a property of samples, not a global axis of cell state.

### Step 10 — Clustering
Built a k-nearest-neighbour graph on the 30 PCs (k=15, 186,626 undirected edges, a single
connected component) and clustered the cells by Ward hierarchical linkage, cutting the
dendrogram at 18 groups.
**Decision:** use Ward rather than Leiden. Leiden is the field standard and is what the original
paper used, but `leidenalg`, `python-igraph`, `scikit-learn` and `scanpy` are all absent from
this machine and the sandbox had no network access, so none could be installed. Ward is
available in SciPy, is deterministic (no random seed, unlike Leiden), and operates on the same
PC space. **The cluster boundaries will therefore not match the published ones.**
**Decision:** choose k=18 by the plateau in kNN-graph modularity. Modularity rises steeply to
k=18 and is then flat to k=30, so 18 is the smallest k that captures the available community
structure. A subsampled silhouette score disagrees and peaks at k=5; that is expected, because
silhouette rewards compact well-separated blobs (the three immune lineages) whereas modularity
rewards graph communities (cell states within lineages). Modularity is the appropriate criterion
for cell typing.
**Output:** 18 clusters covering all 16,048 cells, sizes 140 to 3,852 (median 424), modularity
0.686, silhouette 0.096. Only 7 of the 18 separate visibly in PC1/PC2; the rest are distinguished
on higher components. **Means:** the cells carry reproducible group structure well above what a
random graph would give (Q=0.686 against ~0 for random), but the clusters are not yet identified
as cell types — that is step 12 — and three caveats carry forward: the Ward-for-Leiden
substitution, the 153 receptor V(D)J genes still in the feature set, and PC1's partial
confounding with library complexity.

### Step 11 — Embedding (deferred, not skipped)
Not run. **Decision:** defer. A 2-D embedding needs `umap-learn` or `scikit-learn` (t-SNE) and
neither is installed; the sandbox had no network access, so neither could be installed, and SciPy
has no substitute. Step 12 needs only marker expression, so it was run first.
**Means:** there is currently no 2-D cluster map. This affects presentation only — clustering
(step 10) and annotation (step 12) both operate in the 30-PC space and do not depend on an
embedding.

### Step 12 — Cell-type annotation
Computed per-cluster mean expression and detection fraction for all 45,686 genes, ranked
one-vs-rest enrichment, and scored a 72-marker curated panel covering T/NK, cycling, B/plasma,
myeloid, dendritic and non-immune populations.
**Decision:** annotate from a curated panel plus unbiased top-enriched genes, rather than
automated reference mapping, because no annotation package is installed and the marker evidence
is unambiguous at this granularity.
**Output:** all 18 clusters identified — 8 T/NK (12,704 cells, 79.2%), 4 B/plasma (1,765, 11.0%)
and 6 myeloid/DC (1,579, 9.8%). Populations recovered include cytotoxic CD8 T (CD8A/CCL5/GZMA),
memory/naive T (IL7R/TCF7/CCR7), γδ/effector T (TRDC), Treg (FOXP3 2.69, CTLA4, TIGIT, TNFRSF4/18),
proliferating T (MKI67 5.63), NK (KIR2DL3 4.86, NCAM1, no CD3), naive and memory B, plasma cells
(MZB1 9.57), pDC (LILRA4 10.01), cDC2 (CD1C/FCER1A) and three macrophage states including an
SPP1-high tumour-associated population (SPP1 13.51).
**Verification:** two independent checks. The CD45+ sort is confirmed — the highest cluster-mean
for `PMEL` is 0.20 and for `MLANA` 0.10, so no melanoma cells survived into the dataset, as the
protocol intends. And the T/NK-dominant 79/11/10 split matches what is expected of sorted
tumour-infiltrating leukocytes; a myeloid- or tumour-dominated result would have indicated a
sort or parsing failure.
**Means:** the clusters are now biologically named and step 13 can ask about composition and
state per cell type. Two findings to carry: clusters 6 and 13 (873 cells, 5.4%) are defined
almost entirely by `TRBV7-*` and `TRBV6-*` segments — the clonotype confound predicted in step 8,
now demonstrated rather than suspected, and these two should be merged into their parent CD8
population or the V(D)J genes removed and clustering repeated. High immunoglobulin content in the
plasma-cell and B clusters is *not* an artifact; for those cells it is the correct biology.

### Steps 8–10 and 12 revised — receptor segments excluded
Re-ran feature selection, PCA, clustering and annotation with T/B-cell receptor V, D and J
segments removed from the feature set, after step 12 demonstrated that two clusters (873 cells)
were defined by TCR V-gene families rather than cell state.
**Decision:** exclude only V/D/J *segments* (360 genes, matched by requiring a digit after the
V/D/J letter) and **keep the constant regions** — `IGHM`, `IGHD`, `IGKC`, `IGHG1`, `TRAC`,
`TRBC2`. Constant regions encode isotype and lineage and are what distinguishes a plasma cell
from a naive B cell, so removing them would delete real signal rather than noise.
**Decision:** select k by an explicit rule — the smallest k whose modularity is within 1% of the
maximum — rather than by eye. Applied to the previous sweep the same rule returns k=18, the value
chosen manually, so the rule is not tuned to this run. It gives **k=20** here.
**Output:** 0 of 2,000 HVGs are now receptor segments (was 153); the maximum V(D)J share of any
cluster's top-50 markers fell from 26% to 4%, median 0%. PCA improved slightly (PC1 3.66% vs
3.31%, 30 PCs 12.5% vs 11.8%). 20 clusters, sizes 62–3,970, modularity 0.685, single connected
graph component.
**Verification:** lineage composition is essentially unchanged — T/NK 78.9%, B/plasma 11.0%,
myeloid/DC 10.1%, against 79.2/11.0/9.8% before. That is the check that matters: removing 360
genes should refine substructure without moving the major lineages, and a shifted composition
would have indicated the exclusion had deleted genuine cell-type signal (which is exactly what
dropping the constant regions would have done).
**Means:** the clusters now describe T-cell *functional states* rather than clonotypes — memory
(IL7R/TCF7), exhausted (GZMK/PD-1/HAVCR2), heat-shock, interferon-stimulated, KIR+, proliferating,
CXCL13+ CD4 (Tfh-like), Treg (FOXP3 5.03, up from 2.69) and NK. This is the granularity the
original study reported, and it is the right input for step 13. One caveat: cluster 19
(inflammatory monocyte) holds 62 cells, too few for reliable per-sample proportions.

### Step 13 — Comparative analysis: baseline composition vs response
Compared the cell-type composition of responder and non-responder tumours before treatment.
**Decision:** restrict to **unsorted pre-treatment samples**. All nine sort-enriched samples turn
out to be post-treatment, so the pre-treatment set is unsorted by construction and the enrichment
confound disappears rather than needing adjustment. This leaves 19 samples (9 responder, 10
non-responder; 5,827 cells), which is the contrast that asks whether baseline immune composition
*predicts* response.
**Decision:** use the **sample** as the unit of analysis, not the cell. Each of the 19 samples
comes from a distinct patient (verified: 19 samples, 19 patient IDs), so the samples are
independent. Testing across the 5,827 cells instead would treat cells from one tumour as
independent replicates and inflate significance by orders of magnitude.
**Decision:** Mann-Whitney U (exact, two-sided) per cell type with Benjamini-Hochberg correction
across the 19 tests, reporting Cliff's δ as effect size. Non-parametric because proportions are
bounded and skewed at n≈10 per arm. The 62-cell inflammatory monocyte cluster was merged into
the main monocyte cluster.
**Output:** two cell types pass q<0.05 and three more sit at q=0.050.
**Memory T cells (IL7R/TCF7+) are 44.9% of cells in responder tumours vs 14.9% in
non-responders** (δ=+0.93, p=0.0002, q=0.003) — near-complete separation of the two groups.
**Proliferating T cells (MKI67+) are depleted in responders**, 0.9% vs 5.1% (δ=−0.80, q=0.020).
At q=0.050: naive B cells higher in responders (4.0% vs 0.3%), pDCs and CD14+ monocytes lower.
Exhausted CD8 T cells trend lower in responders (11.8% vs 24.7%) but do not survive correction
(q=0.195).
**Verification / comparison to the literature:** the TCF7 result is a recapitulation, not a new
claim. Sade-Feldman et al. reported that the frequency of TCF7+ CD8 T cells in the tumour
distinguishes responders from non-responders; that is a *quoted* result from the paper, and the
δ=+0.93 above is our own independent measurement from the GEO matrix through this pipeline. The
pipeline reproducing the study's central finding from raw data is the strongest end-to-end check
available here.
**Means, and what it does not mean:** baseline abundance of memory-phenotype T cells separates
these two groups strongly. Four caveats. (i) n=19 patients — the exact test can reach p≈4e-5 at
this size, so q=0.003 is real, but the five borderline hits at q=0.050 should be treated as
suggestive. (ii) Proportions are **compositional** and sum to 100%, so a 30-point gain in one
type mechanically forces losses elsewhere; memory-T and exhausted-CD8 fractions correlate at
−0.43 across samples, so the exhausted-CD8 depletion may be partly arithmetic rather than
independent biology. (iii) Therapy is unbalanced within the set (8 of 10 non-responders on
anti-PD1 alone vs 4 of 9 responders) and was not adjusted for. (iv) This is an association with a
binary response label, not a survival analysis, and not a causal claim.

